/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Overall, this scenario analysis demonstrates the importance of preparing for uncertainty with a strong risk mitigation framework. The project shows potential under moderate to optimistic scenarios but faces significant challenges in crisis conditions. The use of multiple scenarios offers not only numerical insights but also a strategic lens through which the investment decision can be evaluated more robustly. The clarity of variation across metrics - NPV, IRR, BCR, and Payback - under the structured assumptions makes it easier to communicate financial viability and risks to investors, stakeholders, and funding partners in a credible and transparent way.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
544 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (2.4. Monte Carlo Simulation \(Crystal Ball\))
/E (2.4. Monte Carlo Simulation \(Crystal Ball\))
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
545 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This section presents a risk-adjusted financial evaluation of the investment project using Monte Carlo simulation techniques. The analysis uses the Oracle Crystal Ball to assess the impact of uncertainty on key financial performance indicators, including Net Present Value \(NPV\), Benefit-Cost Ratio \(BCR\), and Payback Period \(PP\). Rather than relying on deterministic projections, this approach integrates probabilistic modeling to simulate a broad range of outcomes under real-world volatility. Historical data was first collected and fitted to appropriate probability distributions to reflect the behavior of critical input variables. The selected inputs include standard large kitchen rental prices, special medium kitchen prices, and initial occupancy rate in year 1 - variables which collectively represent a significant portion of total project revenue and early-stage financial viability. These fitted inputs were standardized before being deployed in simulation, ensuring statistical consistency and representativeness in forecast modeling.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
546 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Input variables were chosen based on both their financial materiality and historical volatility. Kitchen rental price was prioritized given its high sensitivity to location and service tier, and because it directly influences projected revenues across all forecast years. The general price for all unit types was similarly included to capture market diversity and price range fluctuation across offerings. Meanwhile, the occupancy rate in year 1 was integrated as an early revenue driver, influencing both cash inflows and cost recovery speed. Each of these variables demonstrated non-trivial distribution spreads, making them suitable for probabilistic modeling. The selection process was guided by the magnitude of their cumulative impact on variance observed in previous deterministic sensitivity tests.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
547 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (The simulation was conducted with 1,000 trials, and the financial forecasts for each scenario were aggregated and analyzed. The distribution-based outputs for each performance metric are summarized below, focusing on the average result, the simulation certainty, and the full range of possible values produced by the Monte Carlo runs.)
/E (The simulation was conducted with 1,000 trials, and the financial forecasts for each scenario were aggregated and analyzed. The distribution-based outputs for each performance metric are summarized below, focusing on the average result, the simulation certainty, and the full range of possible values produced by the Monte Carlo runs.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [4]
>>
